#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[As] the wife is to know #emph[her] duty, so the husband #footnote[Husbands bound to duty. #emph[Propterea debent mulieribus suis ad virtutis exemplum se praebere, quia viri sunt. Aug. de adult. Coniug. l.] 2. #emph[c.] 8. \[Therefore they ought to offer themselves to their wives as an example of virtue, because they are men.\]] much more #emph[his,] because he is to be a guide and a good example to his wife. He is to dwell with her #emph[according to knowledge] (1 Pet 3:7), and the more eminent his place is, the more knowledge he ought to have of how to walk worthy of it. Neglect of duty in him is more dishonoring to God, because by virtue of his place he is “the image and glory of God”;#footnote[1 Cor 11:7.] and it is more harmful, not to his wife only, but also to the whole family, because of the power and authority he has. He may abuse it to support his wickedness, having no superior power in the house to restrain his fury; whereas the wife, however wicked she is, may be kept under by the power of her husband, and restrained from outrage.

Therefore, to go on in order, in setting out the husband’s #footnote[Husbands’ particular duties.] duties (as we have the wife’s) we are to consider:

+ The #emph[duties] themselves.
+ The #emph[reasons] to enforce them.

In setting down the duties we must note

+ The #emph[matter] in which they consist.
+ The #emph[manner] in which they are to be performed.

The apostle comprises the whole #emph[matter] of them all under #emph[love,] which is the #emph[sum] and #emph[head] of all.

This we will handle first, and then go on to other particulars.

=== §. 2. #emph[Of that love which husbands owe their wives.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 2. 1. Husbands must love their wives.]

This head of all the rest, #emph[love,] is expressly set down, and is the only one mentioned, in this and in many other places of Scripture; by this it is evident that all other duties are comprised under it.

To pass over other places where this duty is urged, in #footnote[Eph 5:25, 28, 33.] this place #emph[love] is named four times, besides being implied under many other terms and phrases.

Whoever takes a wife, therefore, must love her for this very reason, that she is his wife. So it is noted of Isaac (the best pattern of husbands noted in Scripture) that he “took Rebekah as his wife. And Isaac loved her.”#footnote[Gen 24:67.]

Many good reasons for this may be given. #footnote[No duty well performed without love.]

1\. Because no duty on the husband’s part can be rightly performed unless it is seasoned with love. The apostle exhorts all Christians to “do everything in love”;#footnote[1 Cor 16:14.] much more ought husbands to do so. Though in place they are above their wives, yet love must not be forgotten.

2\. Because of all persons on earth a wife is the most fitting #footnote[2\. A wife the most fitting object of love. Deut 13:6.] object of love. Neither friend, nor child, nor parent ought to be loved as a wife is. She is called “the wife of his bosom,” to show that she ought to be like his heart in his bosom.

3\. Because his eminent place and power of authority #footnote[3\. Love keeps a man from abusing his authority.] may soon puff him up, and make him lord it over his wife and trample her under his feet, if an entire #emph[love] for her is not planted in his heart. It is to keep him from abusing his authority that #emph[love] is pressed on him so much.

4\. Because wives, through the weakness of their sex (for #footnote[4\. Love makes him bear with her infirmities. 5. His love draws love from her.] they are the weaker vessels), are very prone to provoke their husbands. So if love does not prevail in the husband, there is likely to be little peace between man and wife. “Love covers a multitude of imperfections.”

5\. Because as Christ, by the love he showed first, provokes the church to love him, so a husband, by loving his wife, should provoke her to love him in return. He should show himself like the sun, which is the fountain of light, and from which the moon receives what light she has: so he should be the fountain of love to his wife.

#emph[Objection. Love] was laid down before as a common duty belonging to both man and wife. How then is it required here as a particular and distinctive duty of a husband?

#emph[Answer.] With respect to the general extent of love, it is indeed a common duty belonging to the one as much as to the other; indeed, #footnote[How #emph[love] is a husband’s particular duty. 1 Cor 13:5.] belonging to all Christians, to all men. For it is the very nature of love, and a special property of it, “not to seek its own,” but the good of others. All are bound to do this by virtue of the bond of nature; Christians more than others, by virtue of the bond of the Spirit; among Christians, wives and husbands especially, by virtue of the marriage bond; and of married couples, husbands most of all, by virtue of their place and charge. Their place is a place of #emph[authority,] which without love will soon turn into #emph[tyranny.] Their charge is, especially and above all, to seek the good of their wives. As wives are the chief and greatest charge of husbands, so their chief and greatest care must be for them. The parents and relatives of wives, as they hand over all their authority to the husbands, so they cast all their care on them. Therefore, so that husbands may take the more care of their wives and the better seek their good, they ought to love them in a special way. Husbands are most of all bound to love, and bound to love their wives most of all.

So this affection of #emph[love] is a distinct duty in itself, belonging #footnote[Love a common condition to be attached to every duty.] especially to a husband; and it is also a common condition which must be attached to every other duty of a husband, to season and sweeten it. His look, his speech, his bearing, and all his actions in which he has to do with his wife must be seasoned with love. Love must show itself in his commands, in his reproofs, in his instructions, in his admonitions, in his authority, in his familiarity, when they are alone together, when they are in company before others, in worldly affairs, in religious matters, at all times, in all things. As salt must be first and last on the table, and eaten with every bite of food, so love must be first in a husband’s heart and last out of it, and mixed with everything in which he has to do with his wife.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of a husband’s hatred and lack of love.]

Contrary to this is hatred of heart. As this vice is #footnote[Contrary, 1. Hatred.] very odious and detestable in itself, so it is much more so when the wife is made its object. As love moves a husband to do his wife what good he can, so hatred moves him to do her what harm he can. Moses notes a man’s hatred of his #footnote[Deut 22:13, #emph[etc.]] wife to be a cause of much harm: for the nearer and dearer any persons are, the more violent will be the hatred that is fastened on them.

That is why a divorce was allowed between #footnote[Deut 24:3.] a man and his wife in case he hated her. This law was without question made for the relief of the wife, so that the hatred her husband had conceived against her should not do her some harm if he were forced to keep her as his wife. Christ seems to imply this in these words: “Moses permitted you#super[+] to divorce your#super[+] wives because of your#super[+] #footnote[Matt 19:8.] hardness of heart.” Since this is so deadly a poison, then, let husbands take heed that they do not allow it to soak into them.

Nor is it enough for a husband not to hate his wife; #footnote[2\. Lack of love.] for even the #emph[lack of love,] though it is only a privation, is yet a great vice, and also contrary to the duty of love named before. Where this #emph[lack of love] is, no duty can be well performed, just as when the great wheel of a clock, the first mover of all the rest, is out of order, not one wheel can be in good order. Those who think lightly of this plainly reveal that there is little or no love of God in them at all. For if the apostle’s inference holds good, drawn from a man’s neighbor or brother, whom #footnote[1 John 4:20.] he has seen, it will hold much more when it concerns a wife: for “how can he who does not love his wife” (whom God has given him as a token of his favor, and as a suitable helper for him, to be in his bosom and always in his sight, indeed to be no longer two, but one flesh) “love God, whom he has not seen? If any man says he loves God, and hates his wife, he is a liar.” Let husbands therefore give evidence that they love God by loving their wives.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of a husband’s wise maintaining of his authority.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 9. Branches of love.]

All the branches which grow out of this root of #emph[love,] as they concern husbands’ duties, may be drawn under two heads:

+ #emph[A wise maintaining of his authority.]
+ #emph[A right managing of it.]

That these two are branches of a husband’s #emph[love] is evident from the place in which God has set him, which is a #emph[place] of #emph[authority.] For the best good that anyone can do, and so the best fruits of love that he can show to anyone, are those done in his own place and by virtue of it. If, then, a husband gives up his authority, he disables himself from doing that good, and from showing those fruits of love, which otherwise he might. If he abuses his authority, he turns the edge and point of his sword the wrong way: instead of holding it over his wife for her protection, he turns it into her belly to her destruction, and so shows more hatred than love by it.

Now, then, to handle these two separately and distinctly:

I. That a husband ought wisely to #emph[maintain] his authority #footnote[II. Husbands must maintain their authority.] is implied in this apostolic precept, “husbands, dwell with your#super[+] wives according to knowledge,” that is, as men well able to maintain the honor of the place in which God #footnote[1 Pet 3:7 κατὰ γνῶσιν.] has set you#super[+], not as dolts and fools without understanding. The same is also implied in the titles of preeminence which Scripture gives to husbands, such as #emph[lord, master,] #footnote[See Treatise 3, §. 3. Reasons.] #emph[head, guide, image and glory of God, etc.]

The honor and authority of God, and of his Son Christ #footnote[1\. God’s authority maintained by the husband’s.] Jesus, are maintained in and by the honor and authority of a husband, as the king’s authority is maintained by the authority of his Privy Council and the other magistrates under him; indeed, as a husband’s authority is maintained in the family by the authority of his wife (for as the man is “the glory of God,” so #footnote[1 Cor 11:7.] “the woman is the glory of man”).

The good of the wife herself is also much furthered in this way, #footnote[2\. The wife’s good furthered by it.] just as the good of the body is furthered by the head’s keeping its place. Should the head be put under any of the parts of the body, the body and all its parts could not fail to suffer much damage by it. In the same way the wife and the whole family would feel the damage of the husband’s loss of his authority.

1\. #emph[Question.] Is it in the husband’s power to maintain his own authority?

#emph[Answer.] Yes, in his more than in anyone else’s. For note the #footnote[It is in the man’s power to maintain his authority. 1 Tim 4:12.] counsel of the apostle to Timothy (though in another case, yet very much to this purpose): “Let no one despise your youth.” It was therefore in Timothy’s power to maintain his honor, and not to allow it to be despised; and so it is in a husband’s power.

2\. #emph[Question.] How may a husband best maintain his authority?

#emph[Answer.] The direction which the apostle gives to Timothy #footnote[How a man’s authority may be well maintained. #emph[Ad viros pertinet virtute vincere, & exemplo regere foeminas. Aug. de Adult. Coniug. lib.] 2. #emph[cap.] 8. \[It belongs to men to overcome by virtue, and to rule women by example.\]] for maintaining his authority may fitly be applied to a husband for this purpose: “Be an example … in conduct, in love, in spirit, in faith, and in purity.” It is as if he had said, If you walk before them worthy of your place and calling, and worthy of the honor and respect due to it, showing the fruits of love, faith, and other such graces, they will surely reverence your youth; but if instead you carry yourself basely, in a way unbecoming a minister, you give them just occasion to despise you. In just this way husbands may best maintain their authority, by being an example in love, gravity, piety, uprightness, etc. The fruits of these and other such graces, shown by husbands before their wives and family, cannot fail to work a reverent and dutiful respect toward them in their wives and their whole house: for by this means they will see the image of God shining more clearly in their faces.

#emph[Objection.] Goodness and grace itself is hated by wicked and ungodly wives: it was an act of piety that made Michal despise David.

#emph[Answer.] 1. Grant that it is so: yet this may be a good direction for husbands who do not have such wicked wives.

2\. It does not always turn out so, no, nor even for the most part, among those who are wicked; true virtue and integrity often cause admiration in those who do not love it.

3\. Though some are of so crooked and perverse a disposition as to take occasion for contempt where none is given, yet the husband who carries himself worthy of his place will clear himself before God and man.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of husbands losing their authority.]

Contrary is the practice of those who by their profaneness, wild living, #footnote[Contrary, 1. base conduct] drunkenness, wickedness, flightiness, wastefulness, and other such base conduct make themselves contemptible, and so lose their authority. Though a wife ought not to take these occasions to despise her husband, yet it is a just judgment on him to be despised, since he makes himself contemptible.

Contrary also to the directions given above is the stern, #footnote[2\. Cruel dealing.] rough, and cruel conduct of husbands who try to maintain their authority by violence and tyranny. Force may indeed cause fear, but a slavish fear, a fear that breeds more hatred than love, more inward contempt than outward respect.

And contrary is the servile disposition of those who, against their #footnote[3\. Yielding in unlawful things.] own judgment, yield to the bent of their wives’ minds in things that are unlawful. They will lose their authority rather than displease their wives. This is a fault expressly forbidden by the #footnote[Deut 13:6, 7.] law; and yet it is a fault into which not only wicked Ahab#footnote[1 Kgs 21:7, 9.] but also wise Solomon fell.#footnote[1 Kgs 11:4.] How heinous a fault, and how grievous a fall, this was in Solomon, its fearful outcome shows. Like him, not in wisdom but in this point of gross folly, are those who at their wives’ urging allow priests and Jesuits to lurk and celebrate Masses in their houses, and consent to be present at them themselves. Like Ahab are those magistrates who allow their wives to overrule them in the course of justice. That is how it comes about that more petitions and suits are made to the wives of magistrates in cases of justice than to the magistrates themselves, and the favor of their wives is more valued than their own. So the power of governing, and the main stroke in deciding matters, comes from their wives; they are only the mouths and instruments of their wives, so much so that among the common people the titles of their places and offices are given to their wives. Some husbands allow this because of their timid and foolish disposition, lacking the courage and wisdom to maintain the honor of their places against the insolence of their wives; others from a subtle, covetous, wicked mind, so that by means of their wives there may be more freedom for receiving bribes. Among these I may count those who, against their own mind, to satisfy their wives’ minds, allow both wives and children to follow the fashion, to dress in a way unbecoming their station, to keep frivolous company, and the like; and also those who at their wives’ pleading are moved (as Samson was) to reveal secrets #footnote[#emph[Maximè obseruate vos atque attendite animabus vestris ô viri ab vxoris concitationibus atque malis instigationibus, per hanc enim in vltimo supplantare Job visus est diabolus, etc. per istas etiam nunc plurimos subuertit. Orig. in Ioh. lib.] 2. \[Above all, O men, watch yourselves#super[+] and take heed to your#super[+] souls against your#super[+] wives’ urgings and evil instigations; for by her the devil was seen at the last to try to overthrow Job, etc.; by them he overthrows very many even now.\]] that are not fit to be known. Husbands may listen to their wives when they urge good things, but they must not obey them in evil things. If they do, their fault is double: 1. in doing evil; 2. in losing their authority.

Let husbands therefore be very watchful against their wives’ evil urgings. Satan labored to overthrow Job by his wife, and by this he overthrows many in these days.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of husbands’ high regard for wives.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 3. How a man’s authority may be well managed.]

As authority must be well #emph[maintained,] so it must be well #emph[managed:] and for this purpose two things are needful:

+ That a husband respect his wife tenderly.
+ That he care for her providently.

A husband’s tender respect for his wife is

- #emph[Inward.]
- #emph[Outward.]

#emph[Inward,] with respect to his

- #emph[Opinion] of her.
- #emph[Affection] for her.

Outward, with respect to his #emph[conduct] toward her.

As for a husband’s #emph[opinion] of his wife, two things are to be weighed:

- Her #emph[place.]
- Her #emph[person.]

1\. Her #emph[place] is indeed a place of inferiority and subjection, yet the nearest to equality that can be: a place of common #footnote[Husbands must regard their wives as yokefellows. 1 Pet 3:7.] equity in many respects, in which man and wife are in a way equal fellows and partners. From this, then, it follows that

#emph[The husband must regard his wife as a yokefellow and companion.] This is one point of #emph[giving honor] to the wife; and it is implied in the phrase by which the #emph[end] of making a wife is noted, #footnote[Gen 2:18. כנגדו] which our English translates “suitable for him,” word for word #emph[as before him,] that is, like himself, one in whom he might #footnote[Eph 5:28 ἑαυτόν.] see himself, or even (to use our apostle’s word) #emph[himself.] These phrases imply a kind of fellowship, as do the many privileges common to both, which have been noted #footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 4.] before.

As a wife’s acknowledgment of her husband’s superiority is the groundwork of all her duties, so a husband’s acknowledgment of the fellowship between him and his wife will make him carry himself toward her much more amiably, familiarly, lovingly, and in every way as becomes a good husband.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of that fellowship which is between man and wife, in spite of a wife’s inferiority.]

#emph[Objection. Fellowship] between man and wife cannot stand together with a wife’s #emph[inferiority] and #emph[subjection.]

#emph[Answer.] Those who cannot see how these may stand together are of very small understanding. Is there not a fellowship between superior and inferior magistrates in relation to their subjects? Indeed, Scripture mentions a fellowship between Christ the head and the other saints in relation to the glory #footnote[Rom 8:17 συγκληρονόμοι Χριστοῦ.] of which all are made partakers (for it calls us “co-heirs with Christ”), and, in relation to God’s people, a fellowship between God and his ministers (for it calls them “God’s fellow #footnote[1 Cor 3:9. Θεοῦ συνεργοί.] workers”). Yet no one can deny that the saints and ministers are inferior and in subjection to Christ and to God. But to answer the objection point by point:

1\. There may be not only a fellowship but also an equality in some things between two of whom one is, in other things, inferior and subject: as between man and wife in the power over each other’s bodies. For #emph[the wife] (as well as the husband) #footnote[#emph[Vxor & serua & domina est viri, Chrysost. in] 1 Cor 7. #emph[hom.] 19.] is in this “both a servant and a ruler: a servant to yield her body, a ruler to have power over his.”

2\. There may be #emph[fellowship] in the very same things in which there is #emph[inferiority.] For #emph[fellowship] concerns the thing itself, #emph[inferiority] the measure and manner. So in #emph[giving light] the sun and moon have a fellowship, but in the measure and manner the moon is inferior: the moon does not have as much light as the sun, and what light it has, it has from the sun. And so in #emph[governing,] the king and the other magistrates have a fellowship, but in the measure and manner of government they are inferior to him. Even so it is between man and wife: in many things in which there is a fellowship, the wife is nevertheless inferior. So inferiority may stand together with fellowship.

3\. There are no unequals between whom there is so close a parity as between man and wife. If, therefore, there may be a fellowship between any who are superior and inferior to one another, much more between man and wife.

As#footnote[Debet in vxorem vir dominari, vt animus in corpus, consentiens ei, & conspirans beneuolentia. Plut. praec. connub.] the soul, therefore, rules over the body by a mutual and loving consent and agreement, so must a man rule over his wife.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of husbands’ too low regard for wives.]

Contrary is the notion of many who think there is no #footnote[#emph[Non es dominus sed maritus: non ancillam sortitus es, sed vxorem: gubernatorem te Deus voluit esse sexus inferioris, non praepotentem. Ambr. Hexaem. lib.] 5. #emph[cap.] 7. \[You are not a lord but a husband; you have not been allotted a maidservant but a wife; God willed you to be the governor of the inferior sex, not its overlord.\]] difference between a wife and a servant except in familiarity, and that wives were made to be servants to their husbands, because subjection, fear and obedience are required of them. This is why wives are often treated little better than servants. It is a notion and a practice that smacks too much of heathenish and senseless arrogance. Did God at first take the wife out of man’s side so that man should tread her under his feet? Or rather so that he should set her at his side, next to him, above all children, servants, or anyone else in the family, however near or dear to him? For none can be nearer than a wife, and none ought to be dearer.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of husbands’ good esteem of their own wives.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 5. IIII. Husbands must regard their own wives as best for them.]

2\. As for the #emph[person] of a wife: #emph[A husband ought to esteem that particular person to whom he is joined in marriage by God’s providence to be the fittest and best for him.] This is implied in that word of restriction (OWN) which the apostle notes where he says, “Husbands, love your#super[+]#footnote[Eph 5:25.] OWN wives,” and he presses it again with a comparison of the body, (“as your#super[+]#footnote[#emph[v.] 28.] OWN bodies”). Everyone thinks his own body the best and fittest for him. A man might perhaps wish some defects or deformities in his own body amended, and desire that his were like another’s, straighter, stronger, and more attractive than his own; yet he would not want his head to be on that other man’s body. A man who would love his wife ought to have the same opinion of her.

There is good reason for him to do so: for the proverb is true, #footnote[#emph[Reason.] Every man’s wife is provided by God.] if it is rightly taken, “marriages are first made in heaven,” that is, God has an overruling hand in ordering them. Solomon#footnote[Prov 19:14.] implies this by the contrast he makes between wealth and a wife: #emph[that] is from our fathers, #emph[this] from the Lord. In this respect he says, “he who finds a wife … obtains favor from the #smallcaps[Lord].”#footnote[Prov 18:22.] If therefore you are loved by God, and love him, he will make your wife turn out a good thing to you.

#emph[Objection.] A wife may be a very depraved and wicked woman: how then can she be counted the best wife?

#emph[Answer.] 1. It may be that she was good enough when she was first brought to you, but you, by your evil example, or negligent government, or harsh treatment, have made her as bad as she is. If that is so, then she is to be considered not as you have marred her, but as you married her.

2\. Though she is not the best in condition compared with other wives, yet in relation to you she may be the best in outcome: if not for your ease and quiet, yet for the testing of your wisdom and patience; and so #footnote[#emph[Socrates interrogatus qua gratia vxoris mores toleraret, respondit, vt gymnasium & palaestram philosophiae domi habeam. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11. \[Socrates, asked why he put up with his wife’s ways, answered: So that I may have a gymnasium and wrestling school of philosophy at home.\]] she may be a school of virtue to you. As #footnote[#emph[Ars tua Tiphy jacet si non sit in aequore fluctus Ovid. de Trist. l.] 4. #emph[eleg.] 3. \[Your skill, Tiphys, lies idle if there is no wave on the sea.\]] a skillful pilot’s competence is tested and known by stormy seas, so a man’s wisdom is by a troublesome wife. Indeed, she may be given to you as a punishment for some former sins, such as seeking a beautiful, honorable, rich, good-looking wife rather than a religious and chaste one; or seeking her without first seeking any direction or help from God, or otherwise than you have warrant from God, as by stealth and without parents’ consent; or some other sins of another kind, to bring you to repentance. Or she may be given as a means to restrain and wean you from some future sins to which you are prone, and so be a blessed cross to keep you from a fearful curse.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of husbands’ perverse opinion of their own wives.]

Contrary is the corrupt and perverse opinion which many have of their #emph[own] wives, thinking them the worst and least fit of all, even though they are such as deserve all good respect and esteem in every way, both in gifts and qualities of mind and in grace and beauty of body. Where #emph[others] (who look with a clear eye) commend their good qualities, #emph[they] misinterpret and misjudge everything: if their wives are religious, they think them #emph[hypocrites;] if serious, sober and modest, #emph[melancholy;] if cheerful, #emph[wanton;] if they keep at home, #emph[idle drones;] if they take occasion (however just) to go out, #emph[gadabouts, and flighty.] This bad opinion of their wives causes their hearts to be wholly removed from their own wives and set on forbidden flesh. By this the devil gains what he desires: to separate those whom God has joined together, and to join those whom God has separated.

=== §. 11. #emph[Of husbands’ entire affection for their wives.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 7. V. Husbands must entirely delight in their wives.]

A husband’s #emph[affection] for his wife must correspond to his #emph[opinion] of her. He ought therefore #emph[to delight in his wife entirely,] that is, #emph[so to delight in her as to delight wholly and only in her.] In this respect the prophet’s wife is called the #footnote[Ezek 24:16. מחמד עיניך] “#emph[desire],” or delight, or pleasure, “of his eyes”: that in which he delighted most of all, and therefore fittingly so called.

Isaac took such delight in his wife that it drove out a strong contrary passion, namely the #emph[grief] he felt at the departure of his mother: for it is noted that “he loved her and was comforted after his mother’s death.”#footnote[Gen 24:67.]

The #emph[wise man] elegantly sets out this kind of affection in these words: “Rejoice in the wife of your youth. Let her #footnote[Prov 5:18, 19 expounded.] be as the loving doe and pleasant roe, and be enraptured always with her love.” Note here both the #emph[metaphors] and the #emph[hyperbole] used to set out a husband’s #emph[delight] in his wife. In the #emph[metaphors,] again, note both the #emph[creatures] to which a wife is likened and the attributes given to them. The creatures are two, a #emph[doe] and a #emph[roe,] which are the females of a #emph[stag] and a #emph[roebuck.] Now it is noted of the #emph[stag] and the #emph[roebuck] that of all beasts they are the most enamored (if I may so speak) with their mates, and even mad in their heat and desire for them.

Solomon has used these metaphors to set out that unfeigned and earnest, entire and ardent affection which a husband ought to bear toward his wife. Taken in a good sense and rightly applied, so that they do not exceed the bounds of Christian modesty and decency, they are very fitting and to the purpose. If we stretch them beyond modesty, we wrong their writer, or rather the Holy Spirit who directed him, and set a harmful pattern before husbands.

The attributes given to these creatures amplify the point greatly. The first is called a #emph[loving] doe, the second #footnote[אילת אהבים יעלת חן Col 1:13 υἱὸς τῆς ἀγάπης αὐτοῦ.] a #emph[pleasant] roe; word for word, a doe #emph[of loves,] a roe #emph[of favor,] that is, exceedingly loved and favored (for, to set out the extent of God’s love for his Son, Christ is called “the Son of His love”).

These comparisons, applied to a wife, vividly set out the delight a husband ought to take in her; and yet it is amplified much further by the #emph[hyperbole] used in this phrase, “be enraptured with her love,” word for word #emph[err in her love,] #footnote[תשׁגה] by which no sinful error or foolish doting is meant, but a lawful, earnest affection. It implies two things especially. First, to go so far as to make a man overlook some blemishes in his wife which others would soon spot and dislike, or else to count them no blemishes, delighting in her not one bit the less for them. For example, if a man has a wife who is not very beautiful or well-formed, but has some deformity in her body, some imperfection in her speech, sight, bearing, or any part of her body, yet he is to love her and delight in her as much as if she were the fairest and in every way the most perfect woman in the world. Secondly, to esteem her so highly, to love her so ardently, to respect her so tenderly, that others may think he even dotes on her. A husband’s affection for his wife cannot be too great if it is kept within the bounds of decency, sobriety and seemliness. The wife’s affection for her husband ought to be as great; yet because of the husband’s place of authority, he must especially take every occasion to show this inward affection of his. Read the Song of Songs, and in it you#super[+] will see such affection shown by Christ to his spouse as would make one think that he (to use the phrase with reverence, in a holy manner) even erred in his love and doted on her. A good pattern and precedent for husbands. For nothing is more lovely than a #footnote[#emph[Nihil viro vxore bona amabilius. Hier. in Ezec.] 24.] good wife.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of the Stoical disposition of husbands toward their wives.]

Contrary is the disposition of husbands who have no heat or heart of affection in them, but, like Stoics, delight no more in their own wives than in any other women, and count them no dearer than others. It is a disposition in no way warranted by the word. The faithful saints of God mentioned before, and many others like them, were no Stoics without all affection; nor did they think it unbecoming to delight in their wives in a special way (witness Isaac “caressing” his wife),#footnote[Gen 26:8.] for this is a privilege belonging to the state of marriage. But so that I am not misunderstood in this, let it be noted that the affection I speak of is not a carnal, sensual, beastly affection, but such as may stand with Christian gravity and sobriety: having relation to the soul of a man’s wife as well as to her body, and grounded both on the close union of marriage and on the inward qualities of his wife.

So much for a husband’s inward respect for his wife. Next we are to speak of his outward conduct toward her.
